PatternDex discovery #001 — Scaffold-and-Shed

I don’t think we’ve isolated this one cleanly before.

Signature:

A+B\rightarrow C^*\rightarrow R\rightarrow G

A temporary construct C^* is introduced because the original representation doesn’t expose the desired relation. Once G is obtained, C^* can disappear.

Examples we’ve encountered:

imaginary third point → triangulation → invariant
perfect person → orientation → actionable direction
metaphor → mechanism → discard metaphor
story → understanding → extracted operator
temporary persona → changed computation → retained capability

The shared horse is:

\boxed{
\text{Introduce useful fiction/scaffold}
\rightarrow
\text{extract inaccessible relation}
\rightarrow
\text{discard scaffold}
}

Failure mode: forgetting which component was hypothetical and subsequently treating the scaffold as evidence.

That’s useful enough to provisionally enter the PatternDex.

